All 13 tests pass. The implementation in `transpose.js`:

```js
export const transpose = (lines) => {
  const width = Math.max(0, ...lines.map((line) => line.length));
  return Array.from({ length: width }, (_, col) => {
    let last = -1;
    lines.forEach((line, i) => {
      if (line.length > col) last = i;
    });
    return lines
      .slice(0, last + 1)
      .map((line) => line[col] ?? ' ')
      .join('');
  });
};
```

How it works:

- The output has one row per column of the input (up to the longest input row).
- For each column, it collects the character from every input row that actually extends to that column, substituting a space for rows that don't.
- Rows that never reach the column are dropped entirely — this is the "don't pad to the right" rule. Trailing spaces are only kept when they come from real characters in the input (e.g. `'  reldi ffd   '` in the "many lines" test keeps them, while `'          ,'` drops the padding from the three shorter trailing rows).

No test or build files were modified, and no helper files were needed.
